["18:50"]={
{"Finally a voice said, “Neither Mr. Clifton nor your daughter are on that flight, Mr. Ghuman, and the boarding desk has already closed. Do you want me to check the \00318:50\004 flight?”","Cometh the Hour","Jeffrey Archer",true},
{"At \0036:50 that evening\004, Lacey stood in the atrium dressed in shorts, a blue tank top, and sturdy tennis shoes, ready for a warm evening of walking about. She stared at the tiny red tree frog crouched on a branch at eye level as tour groups and a never-ending stream of smaller parties trooped around her, all wandering the portions of the zig that Swain had opened to the public for the weekend’s open house.","The Enclave","Karen Hancock",true},
{"At \003ten minutes to seven\004 Dulcie was ready. She looked at herself in the wrinkly mirror. The reflection was satisfactory. The dark blue dress, fitting without a wrinkle, the hat with its jaunty black feather, the but-slightly-soiled gloves—all representing self- denial, even of food itself—were vastly becoming. Dulcie forgot everything else for a moment except that she was beautiful, and that life was about to lift a corner of its mysterious veil for her to observe its wonders. No gentleman had ever asked her out before. Now she was going for a brief moment into the glitter and exalted show.","The Four Million","O. Henry",true},
{"It was time to go see the Lady. When we arrived at her house at \003ten minutes before seven\004 o’clock, Damaronde answered the door.","Boy’s Life","Robert R. McCammon",true},
},
["18:51"]={
{"The square of light in the kitchen doorway had faded to thin purple; his watch said \0036:51\004.","Salem’s Lot","Stephen King",true},
},
["18:52"]={
{"As they passed the common a quick glance at the clock in the Town Hall gave them the time. It was just \003eight minutes to seven\004.\n“You’ll be cold,” said Tom, as he noted that Willard had no overcoat on.\n“No, I won’t; not very. There wasn’t time to find anything.” Willard dropped his voice. “How badly is he hurt, Tom?”","Benton’s Venture","Ralph Henry Barbour",true},
{"We’ve missed the train! It’s ten —\003eight— minutes to seven\004, said Arthur, in a voice which combined reproach and terror. And he sprang out of bed and began with hysteric fury to sort out his garments.","Catching the Train","Arnold Bennett",true},
{"It was \003near on seven o’clock\004 when I got to Mr. and Mrs. Fleming’s house on 6th Street, where I was renting a room. It was late September, and though there was some sun left, I didn’t want to visit a dead man’s place with night coming on.","All Aunt Hagar’s Children","Edward P. Jones",true},
},
["18:53"]={
